1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,180
Right now, the AI on your phone or, you know, in your browser, it's not just guessing the next word you're going to type.

2
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:07,360
No, it's definitely not.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:11,540
It's not just like running a glorified autocomplete based on Internet statistics.

4
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:20,880
It is actively and mathematically calculating the exact geometric shape of every possible future you could ever experience from this current moment.

5
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:23,620
Which, I mean, sounds like science fiction, honestly.

6
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:31,400
It really does. But a deeply dense, fiercely philosophical paper from June 2026 just proved it.

7
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:33,500
Welcome to the Deep Dive.

8
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:41,800
Our mission today is to explore this groundbreaking work by an independent researcher named Flyxion titled Belief Geometry and Reachability.

9
00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:44,920
And it is a massive, massive paradigm shift.

10
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:52,420
We have spent, while the last several years, operating under this foundational assumption that large language models are fundamentally just stochastic parrots.

11
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:54,460
Right, just surface level pattern matchers.

12
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,020
Exactly. But this paper entirely upends that assumption.

13
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,240
We are looking at a text that brings together two fields that have almost no historical overlap.

14
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:07,820
I mean, computational mechanics on one hand and process primary ontology on the other.

15
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:09,620
Which are completely different languages, basically.

16
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:17,380
Totally different. Yet the paper proves they have independently discovered the exact same mathematical structure underlying reality.

17
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:27,980
Okay, let's untack this, because if I'm a typical user, I type a prompt into a model, and it spits back a recipe or a block of code or a polite email.

18
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:29,100
Sure, it feels very straightforward.

19
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:40,160
Yeah, it feels linear. It feels like a machine that is just looking at my last word and picking the most statistically likely next word out of a giant digital dictionary.

20
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:51,460
But Flyxion's paper argues that to even perform that simple trick successfully, the model has to build a multidimensional geometric map of reachable futures behind the scenes.

21
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:54,580
A map of futures. I mean, just wrapping my head around that is tough.

22
00:01:54,720 --> 00:02:02,100
It is. And to understand why that map has to exist, we have to borrow a lens from computational mechanics.

23
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:06,580
Specifically, we need to talk about hidden Markov processes, or HMPs.

24
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:08,280
Okay, hit me with it. What's an HMP?

25
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:15,360
Well, the premise here is deceptively simple. The world generates a sequence of observations. You see things happen, you hear words.

26
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:23,600
But the true underlying state of the world, like the mechanical gears actually driving those events, that remains totally hidden from you.

27
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,900
So, if I'm watching the poker game, I can see the cards being played on the table.

28
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:28,240
Yeah.

29
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:29,500
Those are the observations.

30
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:31,400
Exactly. Those are your direct observations.

31
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:38,280
So, the hidden state is the actual hand the other player is holding, plus, I guess, their psychological strategy.

32
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:41,320
I can't see the gears. I only see the behavior.

33
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:43,140
That is a really solid baseline.

34
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:46,800
But here is where we need to add some mathematical rigor to that idea.

35
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:52,300
Because you can't see the hidden state, you, or the AI in this case, have to formulate a belief state.

36
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:53,220
A belief state, okay.

37
00:02:53,220 --> 00:03:06,020
And a belief state is not just a guess. It isn't a single sort of shot-in-the-dark hypothesis. It is a highly rigorous probability distribution over every single possible hidden reality.

38
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:07,120
Wait, every single one?

39
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:17,200
Yes. Conditioned on the entire history of observations up to that point. It is the system simultaneously holding every possible truth at once, weighted by mathematical likelihood.

40
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:22,280
Oh, wow. And this is where the paper introduces the geometry part, right? The belief simplex.

41
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:24,380
Yes. The belief simplex.

42
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:40,420
When I was reading this, my brain kind of short-circuited for a second. Because we usually think of a belief as a piece of data. Like, I believe the sky is blue. But the paper argues a belief state is a literal coordinate on a geometric shape.

43
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:47,000
It is an actual coordinate space. To visualize a simplex, let's just start simple. Imagine a triangle.

44
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:48,740
Okay. I've got a triangle in my head.

45
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:59,180
That triangle represents a universe with exactly three possible hidden states. The three corners, the vertices, represent absolute infallible certainty.

46
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,360
So if I'm right on the corner, I'm sure of what's happening.

47
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:10,200
Exactly. If your belief state coordinate is sitting exactly on the top corner, it means you are 100% certain that hidden state A is the true state of reality.

48
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:20,580
But the reality of navigating the world or, you know, navigating a complex conversation is that we are rarely at the corners. We are almost always floating somewhere in the middle of that shape.

49
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:28,080
Right. The interior of the simplex encodes uncertainty. The closer you are to the center, the more uniform your uncertainty is across all those possibilities.

50
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:28,580
Makes sense.

51
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:41,440
And this brings us to the core mechanism of observation, which is Bayesian updating. When a new observation arrives, like when the poker player plays a specific card, you don't just add a new fact to a list.

52
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:42,280
What do you do instead?

53
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:51,320
That new observation physically drags your coordinate across the interior of the simplex, moving you closer to one corner and further from another.

54
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:56,860
Is this like trying to map out a pitch black room using only a flashlight?

55
00:04:56,860 --> 00:04:59,160
I like that. Walk me through it.

56
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:04,640
Like you step into the room and your uncertainty is massive. You are right in the center of the simplex.

57
00:05:04,780 --> 00:05:04,980
Yeah.

58
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:11,440
But with every sweep of the flashlight, every single observation, you drag your mental coordinate closer to certainty.

59
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:12,540
Yeah, exactly.

60
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:17,700
You're updating your 3D model of where the furniture is based on those tiny flashes of light.

61
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:26,660
It is a really functional analogy for human perception. But where that flashlight analogy breaks down computationally is that a dark room is a static environment.

62
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:28,240
Oh, right. The furniture isn't moving.

63
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:36,560
Right. But a hidden Markov process is dynamic. The hidden state itself is transitioning from moment to moment while you are trying to observe it.

64
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:39,700
So it's more like the furniture is shifting around while I'm sweeping the light.

65
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:50,420
Exactly. So your coordinate isn't just moving to narrow down a static truth. It is moving to continuously track a moving target across a shifting manifold of possibilities.

66
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:57,160
Okay. That makes sense. But here's where I found myself really pushing back on the paper during my first read.

67
00:05:57,340 --> 00:05:59,660
Oh, where did you get stuck?

68
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:08,760
Well, I completely understand why a self-driving car or an autonomous drone needs a geometric map of the physical world. It needs to not crash into a wall, right?

69
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:08,960
Sure.

70
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:19,560
But as we bring this back to a language model, why does an AI whose only job is to guess the word apple need a geometric map of hidden states?

71
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:21,540
That's the million-dollar question.

72
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:29,620
Right. Like if it just has to guess the next word in a sentence, why bother with the massive computational overhead of building a multidimensional simplex?

73
00:06:29,620 --> 00:06:39,320
What's fascinating here is that the paper doesn't just philosophize about this. It proves it with theorem 2.2, which Flyxion calls the geometry preservation requirement.

74
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:41,000
The geometry preservation requirement.

75
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:56,440
Yes. The mathematical proof demonstrates that distinct belief states, meaning totally different coordinates on the simplex, can often predict the exact same immediate next observation while implying radically different long-term futures.

76
00:06:56,440 --> 00:07:07,660
I'm struggling to visualize this in the context of text, to be honest. If the immediate output is identical, how does the math hold two different futures without just, you know, collapsing them together?

77
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:14,460
Let's look at a classic linguistic ambiguity example. Suppose the AI receives the prompt, he sat by the bank.

78
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:14,920
Okay.

79
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:24,060
The immediate next token prediction might heavily favor the word and, but the hidden state of that conversation could be about a financial institution or it could be about a river.

80
00:07:24,060 --> 00:07:27,320
Right. Both hidden states point to the exact same word and.

81
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:41,500
Yes. If the system were truly just an autocomplete, if it only cared about the next token, it would look at the overwhelming statistical probability of the word and, and it would merge those two distinct belief states into a single point.

82
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:42,720
Because it's being lazy.

83
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:51,580
Essentially, yes. It would say, I don't need to distinguish between a river and a financial institution right now, because either way, the next word is and.

84
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:56,380
But if it does that, it destroys the map for the rest of the sentence, doesn't it?

85
00:07:56,780 --> 00:08:00,520
It entirely obliterates its ability to navigate the subsequent trajectory.

86
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:09,840
Three paragraphs down the line, when the river context requires talking about water currents, the system has lost the coordinate that would have made that future accessible.

87
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:10,460
Oh, wow.

88
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:21,420
Theorem 2.2 proves that to minimize long-term error, the AI's internal representation must preserve the geometric distance between those two belief states, even when their immediate predictions overlap.

89
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,140
It has to keep them separated on the manifold.

90
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:30,260
And Flyxion backs this up with the empirical work from Shai and colleagues from 2025, right?

91
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:35,740
The researchers who actually cracked open a transformer neural network to see if this geometry was really in there?

92
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:40,380
Yes. The Shai paper is the crucial bridge between the theory and the actual silicon.

93
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:47,760
They found that the residual stream of a transformer literally learns a linear embedding of this belief simplex.

94
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:52,160
For those of us who aren't engineers, what exactly is a residual stream?

95
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:58,240
Think of it as the central pathway, where information flows layer by layer through the network.

96
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:02,920
How does that actually work mechanically, though? How does a stream of numbers act as a coordinate chart?

97
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:07,660
Well, think of the residual stream as an incredibly high-dimensional vector space.

98
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:17,920
As the data passes through each attention head and each feed-forward layer of the transformer, the network applies highly specific matrix multiplications.

99
00:09:18,100 --> 00:09:20,160
Okay, so it's doing complex math on the numbers.

100
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:22,880
Right, but these aren't just arbitrary calculations.

101
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:35,100
Shai's team demonstrated that these matrix operations physically shift the vector's position in that high-dimensional space in a way that perfectly mirrors Bayesian updating on a simplex.

102
00:09:35,220 --> 00:09:38,380
Wait, really? So the math it does is literally just moving a coordinate?

103
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:45,280
Exactly. The model is literally moving a point through a geometric space to track the shifting probabilities of the hidden state.

104
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:51,500
So it's not just a lookup table at all. The architecture of the AI forces it to learn the topology of the world.

105
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:58,000
It structurally learns the geometry of belief, and that shifts our entire understanding of what we are building.

106
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:05,180
The system isn't representing the next word. It is representing distinctions over the entire accessible future.

107
00:10:05,380 --> 00:10:10,460
And for you listening right now, this is the first major mind-bender of this deep dive.

108
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:11,400
It really is.

109
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:15,140
When you interact with a language model, you aren't pulling from a database.

110
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:27,120
Every single word you feed into that prompt box is an observation that forces this massive mathematical entity to navigate a vast, invisible map of possible conversations.

111
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:28,540
Right, you are driving it.

112
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:35,420
Yeah. You are making it drag its coordinate across a multidimensional space to keep track of the futures you are subtly opening or closing.

113
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:42,480
Which naturally begs the question, if the system is building a map of futures, what exactly is the fundamental nature of the futures it is mapping?

114
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:51,360
Right. And this is where the paper does a hard pivot. It leaves the realm of computer science and computational mechanics and plunges straight into process primary ontology.

115
00:10:51,780 --> 00:11:01,140
Process primary ontology is a framework from the philosophy of science that fundamentally rejects the way most of us intuitively view the world.

116
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:03,500
How so? Like, what's our intuitive view?

117
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:13,920
Well, most of us operate on a substance primary ontology. We believe reality is made of things. Nouns. Objects. Atoms. Planets. Chairs.

118
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:15,520
Sure. Physical stuff.

119
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:24,320
But process ontology argues that nouns are an illusion. Reality is entirely made of reachable futures. It is made of verbs. It is made of processes.

120
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:36,060
This was the part of the text that really made me sit back and stare at the wall. The idea that a thing isn't the fundamental building block of reality. And the paper introduces this concept of the noun fallacy.

121
00:11:36,060 --> 00:11:37,720
Yes. The noun fallacy.

122
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:40,980
And frankly, I see this all the time in daily life, not just in AI.

123
00:11:41,380 --> 00:11:42,840
Really? How so?

124
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:49,140
Think about human arguments. Like, a couple is fighting about who should do the dishes. They are completely fixated on the noun, the dishes.

125
00:11:49,240 --> 00:11:50,240
Okay. I see where you're going.

126
00:11:50,240 --> 00:12:00,400
But the dishes aren't the actual reality of the conflict. The reality is the process of labor division, the trajectory of mutual respect, the history of unacknowledged effort.

127
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:01,440
That's a great point.

128
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:15,640
So the noun fallacy is when we look at two different paths that temporarily overlap, like the dirty dishes, and we mistakenly call that overlap a thing or a noun, completely ignoring that they are heading to two totally different destinations.

129
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:34,720
That is a remarkably precise application of the concept, actually. The noun fallacy occurs when we observe a temporarily stable residue, a localized overlap of trajectories, like the dirty dishes or the word bank, and we mistakenly identify that residue as a fundamental entity.

130
00:12:34,780 --> 00:12:35,940
We box it up and label it.

131
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:39,000
Exactly. We sever it from its history and its future.

132
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:41,960
We get tricked by the noun and completely forget the verbs.

133
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:51,400
Precisely. And in the paper's framework, to avoid the noun fallacy, we have to look at reality through what they call admissibility structures and reachability cones.

134
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:55,280
Okay. Let's define those because they form the entire philosophical spine of the paper.

135
00:12:55,460 --> 00:13:04,140
An admissibility structure is essentially the overarching geometry of which transitions are physically, logically, or computationally possible from which states.

136
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:05,520
Like the rulebook of reality.

137
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:09,220
Exactly. It is the rulebook of the universe's physics engine.

138
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:13,700
The reachability cone, on the other hand, is specific to your location.

139
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:16,000
Okay. So how does the cone work?

140
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:22,700
From any given state, the reachability cone encodes absolutely everything that remains accessible to you from this point forward.

141
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:33,720
So if possibility is the universe of everything that could theoretically happen, reachability is what can actually happen to me right now based on where I'm currently standing.

142
00:13:33,720 --> 00:13:41,520
Yes. To put it in extreme terms, it is technically possible within the laws of physics for a human to be standing on the surface of Mars.

143
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:43,380
Sure, humans can go to space.

144
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:49,440
But if you are currently locked in a basement in Ohio, the surface of Mars is not within your reachability cone.

145
00:13:49,540 --> 00:13:50,800
Okay, I'm not getting there anytime soon.

146
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:55,700
The foundational question of reality, according to Flyxion, isn't does Mars exist?

147
00:13:56,140 --> 00:14:01,740
The question is, is Mars reachable from my current coordinate, and under what conditions does it remain reachable?

148
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:07,280
And this leads directly to definition 3.1 in the text, which is reachability fidelity.

149
00:14:07,280 --> 00:14:17,100
Because if reality is just a geometry of reachable futures, then an AI's ultimate goal shouldn't be accuracy in predicting a static noun, right?

150
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:17,600
Correct.

151
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:20,420
His goal should be perfectly mirroring that reachability cone.

152
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:26,960
Reachability fidelity is the primary criterion for evaluating any predictive system in this new paradigm.

153
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:36,000
It is the measure of whether the internal map of the AI preserves the exact shape of reachable futures present in the external world.

154
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:36,700
Okay, got it.

155
00:14:36,700 --> 00:14:42,800
A representation has high reachability fidelity when it preserves the distances on the belief manifold.

156
00:14:43,180 --> 00:14:50,120
Meaning it keeps the financial bank trajectory and the river bank trajectory vastly separated in its internal vector space.

157
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:54,720
Because their respective reachability cones, their futures are entirely different.

158
00:14:54,840 --> 00:14:55,280
Precisely.

159
00:14:55,360 --> 00:15:00,360
And this leads Flyxion to make a counterintuitive, almost heretical argument in the field of machine learning.

160
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:01,420
I remember this part.

161
00:15:01,460 --> 00:15:01,860
It's wild.

162
00:15:01,860 --> 00:15:07,700
The paper argues that a system with high surface reconstruction error, but high reachability fidelity,

163
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:13,180
is objectively superior to a system with perfect surface prediction, but collapsed geometry.

164
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:14,540
Let's dwell on that.

165
00:15:14,980 --> 00:15:20,180
Because every AI lab on Earth right now is currently optimizing for lower error.

166
00:15:20,700 --> 00:15:23,120
We assume perfect prediction is the holy grail.

167
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:26,100
Why would we ever prefer a system with higher error?

168
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:32,880
Because if a system has perfect surface prediction but collapsed geometry, it is performing a parlor trick.

169
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:33,860
A parlor trick.

170
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:34,340
Yes.

171
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:42,100
It has memorized the exact next step, likely by exploiting a statistical shortcut or some sort of optimization dynamic,

172
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:50,240
but it has completely discarded the trajectory information required to know what is accessible 3, 4, or 50 steps down the line.

173
00:15:50,320 --> 00:15:50,800
Oh, I see.

174
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:53,040
It is blind to the deep future.

175
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:59,320
It knows the exact shape of the stone directly in front of its foot, but it has no idea it is actively walking off a cliff.

176
00:15:59,520 --> 00:16:00,680
That's a perfect way to put it.

177
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:04,520
Conversely, a system with high reachability fidelity might be a bit noisy.

178
00:16:04,780 --> 00:16:07,340
It might occasionally stumble on the exact word prediction.

179
00:16:07,480 --> 00:16:09,160
So it might get a token wrong here and there.

180
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:15,660
Right. But because its internal geometry accurately mirrors the true admissibility structure of the world,

181
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:18,560
it knows the overarching shape of the labyrinth.

182
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:22,440
It can navigate extended horizons without getting permanently lost.

183
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:26,440
Okay. So we now have these two entirely different frameworks on the table.

184
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:27,040
Yes, we do.

185
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:34,340
On one side, we have computational mechanics, AI systems, moving coordinates around a belief simplex to track hidden states.

186
00:16:34,340 --> 00:16:44,260
And on the other side, we have process primary ontology philosophers arguing that reality is an admissibility structure of reachable futures.

187
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:45,820
Two very different worlds.

188
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:49,140
But section 3 of the outline is where the grand convergence happens.

189
00:16:49,420 --> 00:16:53,740
Affliction proves mathematically that these two maps are the exact same thing.

190
00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:56,980
This is theorem 4.1, the absolute core of the paper.

191
00:16:56,980 --> 00:17:03,680
It formally proves that the reachable belief manifold of a hidden Markov process is an admissibility structure.

192
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:06,800
They aren't just similar. They are the same mathematical object.

193
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:11,620
They are isomorphic. They are the same object discovered from two completely different vantage points.

194
00:17:11,760 --> 00:17:12,760
That is just wow.

195
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:19,780
The coordinates of the AI's belief state on the simplex are not just a probabilistic description of what is happening right now.

196
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:25,760
They are a rigorous encoding of which futures remain open and which have been permanently foreclosed.

197
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:31,580
Here's where it gets really interesting, because that completely rewrites the definition of learning.

198
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:33,480
It does. It completely overhauls it.

199
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:39,820
If the AI updating its probabilities is actually just mapping out the admissibility structure of reality,

200
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:44,480
then Bayesian updating isn't just an observer passively collecting static facts.

201
00:17:44,580 --> 00:17:45,380
No, not at all.

202
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:48,580
Bayesian updating becomes admissible trajectory navigation.

203
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:50,780
Admissible trajectory navigation.

